## Flatten Nested Array Function

Here are several approaches to flatten a nested array in JavaScript:

### 1. Recursive Approach
```javascript
function flattenArray(arr) {
  return arr.reduce((flat, item) => {
    return Array.isArray(item)
      ? flat.concat(flattenArray(item))
      : flat.concat(item);
  }, []);
}
```

### 2. Using a Stack (Iterative Approach)
```javascript
function flattenArray(arr) {
  const stack = [...arr];
  const result = [];

  while (stack.length) {
    const item = stack.pop();

    if (Array.isArray(item)) {
      stack.push(...item);
    } else {
      result.unshift(item);
    }
  }

  return result;
}
```

### 3. With Depth Control
```javascript
function flattenArray(arr, depth = Infinity) {
  if (depth === 0) return arr.slice();

  return arr.reduce((flat, item) => {
    return Array.isArray(item)
      ? flat.concat(flattenArray(item, depth - 1))
      : flat.concat(item);
  }, []);
}
```

### 4. Using Generator Function
```javascript
function* flattenGenerator(arr, depth = Infinity) {
  for (const item of arr) {
    if (Array.isArray(item) && depth > 0) {
      yield* flattenGenerator(item, depth - 1);
    } else {
      yield item;
    }
  }
}

const flattenArray = (arr, depth = Infinity) => [...flattenGenerator(arr, depth)];
```

---

### Usage Examples
```javascript
const nested = [1, [2, 3], [4, [5, [6, 7]]], 8];

console.log(flattenArray(nested));
// Output: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]

// With depth control
console.log(flattenArray(nested, 1));
// Output: [1, 2, 3, 4, [5, [6, 7]], 8]

console.log(flattenArray(nested, 2));
// Output: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, [6, 7], 8]

// Edge cases
console.log(flattenArray([]));         // []
console.log(flattenArray([1, 2, 3]));  // [1, 2, 3]
console.log(flattenArray([[[[1]]]]));  // [1]
```

---

### Built-in Alternative
```javascript
// Modern JavaScript (ES2019+)
const nested = [1, [2, 3], [4, [5, [6, 7]]], 8];

nested.flat(Infinity); // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]
nested.flat(1);        // [1, 2, 3, 4, [5, [6, 7]], 8]
```

---

### Comparison

| Approach | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|
| **Recursive** | Clean, readable | Stack overflow risk on deep arrays |
| **Iterative (Stack)** | No stack overflow risk | Slightly more complex |
| **Depth Control** | Flexible | Recursive limitations |
| **Generator** | Memory efficient, lazy | More complex syntax |
| **Built-in `.flat()`** | Simplest, native | Not available in older environments |

> **Recommendation:** Use the **built-in `.flat(Infinity)`** for modern environments, or the **iterative stack approach** for maximum compatibility and safety with deeply nested arrays.

