### 3. The Efficient Way: Using Iteration with a Stack

This approach avoids recursion and its potential stack overflow issues by using a stack to manage the nested arrays. It's more complex to write but can handle arbitrarily deep structures without crashing.

```javascript
/**
 * Flattens a nested array using an iterative approach with a stack.
 * This avoids recursion and potential stack overflow errors.
 * @param {Array} arr The array to flatten.
 * @returns {Array} The new flattened array.
 */
function flattenWithStack(arr) {
  const result = [];
  // Start with a copy of the input array as our stack.
  const stack = [...arr];

  while (stack.length > 0) {
    // Get the last element from the stack.
    const next = stack.pop();

    if (Array.isArray(next)) {
      // If it's an array, push its elements onto the stack.
      // The spread operator (...) adds them individually.
      stack.push(...next);
    } else {
      // If it's not an array, add it to our result.
      result.push(next);
    }
