### Advanced: Debounce with "Immediate" (Leading Edge) Option

Sometimes, you want the function to fire *immediately* on the first call and then wait for a cooldown period before it can be fired again. This is useful for things like preventing double-clicks on a "Save" button.

This is called a "leading edge" debounce.

```javascript
/**
 * Creates a debounced function with an option to fire on the leading edge.
 *
 * @param {Function} func The function to debounce.
 * @param {number} delay The number of milliseconds to delay.
 * @param {boolean} [immediate=false] Specify true to invoke on the leading edge.
 * @returns {Function} Returns the new debounced function.
 */
function debounce(func, delay, immediate = false) {
  let timeoutId;

  return function(...args) {
    const context = this;

    // The function to be executed when the timer expires.
    const later = function() {
      // Nullify timeoutId so the next call can be immediate if needed.
      timeoutId = null;
      // If not immediate, execute the function now.
      if (!immediate) {
        func.apply(context, args);
      }
    };

    // Determine if this is the first call in a new sequence.
    const callNow = immediate && !timeoutId;

    // Always clear the timer to reset the delay.
    clearTimeout(timeoutId);

    // Set the new timer.
    timeoutId = setTimeout(later, delay);

    // If it's an immediate call, execute the function right away.
    if (callNow) {
      func.apply(context, args);
    }
  };
}

// --- Example Usage for Immediate ---
