 * milliseconds have elapsed since the last time the debounced function was invoked.
 *
 * @param {Function} func The function to debounce.
 * @param {number} delay The number of milliseconds to delay.
 * @returns {Function} Returns the new debounced function.
 */
function debounce(func, delay) {
  // 1. A variable to hold the timer ID. This variable persists across calls
  //    due to the closure.
  let timeoutId;

  // 2. Return a new function that will be the "debounced" version.
  //    It uses rest parameters to accept any number of arguments.
  return function(...args) {
    // 3. 'this' refers to the context in which the debounced function was called.
    //    We save it to apply it later.
    const context = this;

    // 4. Clear the previous timeout. If the function is called again before the
    //    delay has passed, this will reset the timer.
    clearTimeout(timeoutId);

    // 5. Set a new timeout.
    timeoutId = setTimeout(() => {
      // 6. After the delay, call the original function with the saved context
      //    and arguments.
      func.apply(context, args);
    }, delay);
  };
}
```

#### How It Works (Line-by-Line)

1.  **`let timeoutId;`**: We declare a variable `timeoutId` in the outer function's scope. Because of JavaScript closures, this variable will be "remembered" by the inner function that we return. It will hold the ID of the `setTimeout` timer.

2.  **`return function(...args) { ... }`**: The `debounce` function doesn't execute your code directly. Instead, it *returns a new function*. This new function is the one you will actually use in your event listeners or calls. It acts as a wrapper around your original function.

3.  **`const context = this;`**: Inside the returned function, `this` refers to whatever context the debounced function was called with. For example, in an event listener (`element.addEventListener(...)`), `this` would be `element`. We save it in a `context` variable so we can use it later inside the `setTimeout` callback, where `this` would otherwise be different.

4.  **`clearTimeout(timeoutId);`**: This is the core of the debounce logic. Every time the returned function is called, it first clears the previously scheduled timer (if one exists). This effectively "resets the clock."

5.  **`timeoutId = setTimeout(() => { ... }, delay);`**: After clearing the old timer, we set a new one. We schedule the original function (`func`) to be executed after the `delay` has passed. The ID of this new timer is stored back into `timeoutId`.

6.  **`func.apply(context, args);`**: When the timer finally completes without being cleared, this code runs. It calls your original function (`func`) using `.apply()`. This ensures that the function is executed with the correct `this` context and with the arguments that were passed to the *last* call of the debounced function.

